1. d4 Nf6 2. g3 d5 3. Bg2 Nc6 4. Nf3 Bf5 5. O-O e6 6. c4 Be7 7. Nc3 h6 8. Ne5
O-O 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bf4 Rb8 11. Qd2 g5 12. Be3 Ng4 13. Rfc1 Bg6 14. Na4 Bb4
15. Nc3 Be7 16. Na4 Nxe3 17. Qxe3 Rb4 18. b3 dxc4 19. Rxc4 Rxc4 20. bxc4 Bf6
21. Be4 Bxd4 22. Qd3 Bxe4 23. Qxe4 Qd6 24. Rb1 Bg7 25. Rb3 Qd2 26. Nc5 Qxa2 27.
Kg2 Rd8 28. Rb7 Rc8 29. Qd3 a5 30. Qd7 Rf8 31. Nxe6 fxe6 32. Rxc7 Qb2 33. Qxe6+
Kh8 34. Rc8 a4 35. Qf5 Qb4 (35... Kg8 36. Qe6+ Kh8 37. Qf5 Qb4 38. c5 Rxc8 39.
Qxc8+ Kh7 40. Qf5+ Kg8 41. Qe6+ Kf8 42. Qd6+ Kf7 43. Qd7+ Kg8 44. Qe6+ Kf8 45.
Qc8+ Kf7 46. Qd7+ Kg6 47. Qd3+ {was the finish according to CHESS, May 1952,
p154 & Universities Chess Annual, 3rd issue, 1952-3, p8}) 36. c5 Rxc8 37. Qxc8+
Kh7 38. Qf5+ Kg8 39. Qe6+ Kf8 40. Qd6+ Kf7 {("Abandoned as a draw" - BCM, May
1952, p138)} 41. Qd7+ Kg8 42. Qe6+ Kf8 43. Qc8+ Kf7 44. Qd7+ Kg6 45. Qd3+ {
("Mark Taimanov & I recalled how we had visited Liverpool in the Spring of
1952, invited by the International Union of Students, to take part in the
first Student World Championship ... (never held again) ... so presumably
Taimanov and I, as we tied for first place, are still Co-World Student
Champions!" (Bronstein, "Sorcerer's Apprentice", 1995). N.B. This game was
scheduled for the start of the tournament, on 9 April, but postponed as the
two players did not arrive until the following day. It is believed to have
been played near the end of the tournament.} 1/2-1/2

